{
 "thread": "1aLsQOTZ_4U",
 "subject": "Noticed this problem when determining package dependencies",
 "messages_listed": 2,
 "last_activity": "2015-09-10T21:45:21Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "oIE82v76KgAJ",
   "from": "Jacqueline Buros Novik",
   "author": "c1f498d9cb6627e8",
   "date": "2015-09-02T20:25:03Z",
   "subject": "Noticed this problem when determining package dependencies",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>I have run into a scenario when using packrat::init() and thought I&#39;d post it here in case it helps someone. </div><div><br></div><div>Also, while I understand what&#39;s going on, I wonder if there isn&#39;t a better way to handle this other than to write my code differently ;)  [[ this is always a solution to *any* programming problem ]]</div><div><br></div><div>Anyways. I have an old project &amp; am trying to initialize packrat for this. </div><div><br></div><div>Here&#39;s what I see:</div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"font-family:Consolas,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,monospace;font-size:13.8666667938232px;outline:none;border-style:none;border-width:initial;word-break:break-all;line-height:15.2533330917358px;color:rgb(0,0,0);white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">packrat::init(options = list(local.repos = c(&quot;/analytics/local-cran/alpha&quot;)))\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Initializing packrat project in directory:\r\n- &quot;/u/jburos/new_projects/analysis_explore_####&quot;\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Error in if (result$source == &quot;unknown&quot; &amp;&amp; fallback.ok) { : \r\n  missing value where TRUE/FALSE needed\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In addition: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Warning messages:\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">1: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In value[[3L]](cond) :</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">\r\n </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> Failed to parse /u/jburos/new_projects/analysis_explore_###/examples/example_###_analysis.R ; dependencies in this file will not be discovered.\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">2: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In FUN(X[[i]], ...) :</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">\r\n </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> Package &#39;x&#39; not available in repository or locally\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">3: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In FUN(X[[i]], ...) :</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">\r\n </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> Failed to infer source for package &#39;ggvis&#39;; using latest available version on CRAN instead</span></pre></div><div><pre style=\"font-family:Consolas,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,monospace;font-size:13.8666667938232px;outline:none;border-style:none;border-width:initial;word-break:break-all;line-height:15.2533330917358px;color:rgb(0,0,0);white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"><br></span></pre><pre style=\"font-family:Consolas,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,monospace;font-size:13.8666667938232px;outline:none;border-style:none;border-width:initial;word-break:break-all;line-height:15.2533330917358px;color:rgb(0,0,0);white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Now, warning #2 was the first thing that caught my eye - package &#39;x&#39; ? What is that? </span><br></pre></div><div><br></div><div>Tried to grep by source files for usual suspects, but couldn&#39;t find this pernicious package &#39;x&#39;.</div><div><br></div><div>So, I then did the following to figure out which file packrat thought was using : </div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"outline:none;border-style:none;border-width:initial;word-break:break-all\"><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">files </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dir</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pattern </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;*.R$&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>k </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">()</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">file </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> files</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\">k</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> packrat</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:::</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fileDependencies</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">R</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">file</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><pre style=\"outline:none;border-style:none;border-width:initial;word-break:break-all\"><br><span>\r\n</span></pre></pre>using this, I found the offending bit of code: </div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">packages </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;netezza&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;arm&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;blme&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;ggvis&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;stringr&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;plyr&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;dplyr&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;splines&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;reshape2&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;data.table&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;shiny&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;ggplot2&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;scales&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;gridExtra&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;directlabels&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;lmtest&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;memoise&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>lapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">X </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> packages</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">FUN </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">function</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> suppressPackageStartupMessages</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">library</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">character</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">only </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> quietly </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lib</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">loc </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;/usr/lib64/R/library&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div><div></div><div>Ah - so .. yikes. for now, I suspect I will have to rewrite this part for it to work with packrat. Posting this message here in case it&#39;s helpful to others.</div><div><br></div><div>In the long run, it would be nice to know <i>which files</i> are responsible for packrat&#39;s thinking a particular package is required by code. </div><div><br></div><div>Also, is there a way for me to (a) tell packrat which packages my project depends on, or (b) tell packrat to ignore package &quot;x&quot; ?  </div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "6QtxTQV0LQAJ",
   "from": "Kevin Ushey",
   "author": "ab0d2be2ea8dabe8",
   "date": "2015-09-10T21:45:21Z",
   "subject": "Re: Noticed this problem when determining package dependencies",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Jacqueline,<div><br></div><div>Thanks for the bug report! It looks like packrat needs to be a bit smarter about how it infers dependencies when library is called in this particular way -- I&#39;ll add an issue on GitHub to track this.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div>Kevin</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br><br>On Wednesday, September 2, 2015 at 1:25:03 PM UTC-7, Jacqueline Buros Novik wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>I have run into a scenario when using packrat::init() and thought I&#39;d post it here in case it helps someone. </div><div><br></div><div>Also, while I understand what&#39;s going on, I wonder if there isn&#39;t a better way to handle this other than to write my code differently ;)  [[ this is always a solution to *any* programming problem ]]</div><div><br></div><div>Anyways. I have an old project &amp; am trying to initialize packrat for this. </div><div><br></div><div>Here&#39;s what I see:</div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"font-family:Consolas,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,monospace;font-size:13.8666667938232px;outline:none;border-style:none;border-width:initial;word-break:break-all;line-height:15.2533330917358px;color:rgb(0,0,0);white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"color:blue;white-space:pre\">&gt; </span><span style=\"color:blue\">packrat::init(options = list(local.repos = c(&quot;/analytics/local-cran/alpha&quot;)))\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Initializing packrat project in directory:\r\n- &quot;/u/jburos/new_projects/analysis_explore_####&quot;\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Error in if (result$source == &quot;unknown&quot; &amp;&amp; fallback.ok) { : \r\n  missing value where TRUE/FALSE needed\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In addition: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">Warning messages:\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">1: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In value[[3L]](cond) :</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">\r\n </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> Failed to parse /u/jburos/new_projects/analysis_explore_###/examples/example_###_analysis.R ; dependencies in this file will not be discovered.\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">2: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In FUN(X[[i]], ...) :</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">\r\n </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> Package &#39;x&#39; not available in repository or locally\r\n</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">3: </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">In FUN(X[[i]], ...) :</span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\">\r\n </span><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"> Failed to infer source for package &#39;ggvis&#39;; using latest available version on CRAN instead</span></pre></div><div><pre style=\"font-family:Consolas,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,monospace;font-size:13.8666667938232px;outline:none;border-style:none;border-width:initial;word-break:break-all;line-height:15.2533330917358px;color:rgb(0,0,0);white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"color:rgb(197,6,11)\"><br></span></pre><pre style=\"font-family:Consolas,&#39;Lucida Console&#39;,monospace;font-size:13.8666667938232px;outline:none;border-style:none;border-width:initial;word-break:break-all;line-height:15.2533330917358px;color:rgb(0,0,0);white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><span style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px\">Now, warning #2 was the first thing that caught my eye - package &#39;x&#39; ? What is that? </span><br></pre></div><div><br></div><div>Tried to grep by source files for usual suspects, but couldn&#39;t find this pernicious package &#39;x&#39;.</div><div><br></div><div>So, I then did the following to figure out which file packrat thought was using : </div><div><br></div><div><pre style=\"outline:none;border-style:none;border-width:initial;word-break:break-all\"><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">files </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> dir</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#